+
+### 1.3 Two crashes, fixed by the second patch group — `patches/fixes/0001-known-failures.patch`
+
+These two fixes are **not** in the reference reconstruction behind Petacat's
+published studies. They are the bundle's second, optional patch group
+(`tools/reconstruct.py --fixes`). Each is a send to `#f`, found when the support
+study sampled 969,000 runs and recorded three `*ERROR*` observations (the
+bundle's `KNOWN-FAILURES.md`).
+
+- **`make-group`** (`groups.ss:43-56`) names a letter-category sameness group after
+  its first object's letter category. Building an answer, a translated rule that
+  swaps a one-letter group with a length-based group (`abc → cba; mrrjjj`) leaves
+  a letter-category group whose first object is the length group, which has no
+  letter category, and `get-uppercase-name` is sent to `#f`. The group now gets no
+  letter-category description and no print name, as a length-based group gets
+  none.
+- **`change-descriptions->rule-clause-template`** (`rules.ss:793-805`) asked only
+  the first change description of a reference object for its bond-facet change.
+  A `(self group-category opposite)` change need not come first (the order follows
+  the bridges and the abstraction coin flips); the clause then lacks the
+  bond-facet change, and `transcribe-to-english`, which every new rule goes
+  through, takes `(3rd #f)` (`abc → aabbcc; kkjjii`). The first bond-facet change
+  of any of the descriptions is now used.
+
+**Effect on behaviour: none where they do not fire.** The second always crashed
+when it arose. The first crashed for a sameness group; for a successor or
+predecessor group it would instead have given the group a letter-category
+description with no letter, a branch no recorded run reaches. Recorded runs of
+all 19 demo problems are byte-identical with and without the group.
+
